menu "espOS time"

    config ESPOS_TIME_RTC_STALE_H
        int "A time carried through deep sleep goes stale after (hours)"
        default 24
        range 0 8760
        help
            A device that naps between measurements keeps the wall clock in
            RTC memory and adopts it on waking, so it is not blind until the
            network comes back. That value drifts: the RTC oscillator is not
            a timekeeping crystal, and the sleep itself is only approximately
            measured. Past this age the device still reports the time — it is
            the best guess it has, and a log line with an approximate stamp
            beats one with none — but stops calling itself synced, so a
            consumer that needs a trustworthy timestamp can tell the
            difference.

            0 disables the rule and a restored time never goes stale.

    config ESPOS_TIME_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS
        int "Clock-change subscribers"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
            How many consumers may register with espos_time_subscribe() to be
            told when a source sets the clock. espOS itself takes one for the
            SignalK fallback (to stop it once NTP succeeds); an application
            wanting to redraw a display clock takes another. ~8 bytes each,
            reserved for the life of the firmware.

endmenu
